Neon spaces (M1b): tab groups and splits belong to one space.

A tab group belongs to the space of the first tab that joined it; a tab of
another space that ends up in it (an extension's chrome.tabs.group, for
instance) is taken out again. A split whose tabs are in two spaces is
unsplit, and the split view's tab picker offers only tabs of the split's
space. Keeping a space's tabs together now moves groups and splits as whole
blocks instead of skipping any range that holds one.

A closed saved tab group reopens by URL alone, from the bookmarks bar, the
menu or tab restore. Each window remembers, in the profile, the container of
every open saved group, and the group's tabs reopen there instead of in the
visible space.

Neon code, GPL-3.0.

